{0 Conventions: how the bindings are organised}

{b OUTLINE — to be written by hand.} Each section lists what to cover and
suggested points. The diagrams are drafts to keep, change or drop. Remove
this paragraph and the [TODO] notes when the page is written.

{1 Who this page is for}

This page explains how the bindings are organised. It is meant for both
those familiar with GObject-based libraries like GTK (maybe from C or other languages) 
as well as those who are new to GObject, GTK and OCaml.

{1 Background on GObject}

GObject (or the GLib Object system) defines a portable object model 
for C-based code in a way that makes it straightforward to bind 
and use from other languages. GObject is the cornerstone of GTK
and its dependencies, as well as libraries that depend on it like
GNOME's Adwaita.

At the core of GObject is a generic, introspectable type system
called GType. This runtime description facilitates cross-language
bindings, which can be done either dynamically at runtime (e.g.
in loosely typed languages like JavaScript or Python) or used
to generate metadata for procedurally generating binding code
for strongly typed languages via {b GObject Introspection} 
(like Rust or OCaml) - we've used the latter approach.

The GType system can handle primitives like strings, numbers, booleans,
etc., as well as objects and classes (using a single-parent inheritance
structure but allowing multiple interfaces, similar to Java or Objective-C).
It contains enough metadata to specify how reference counting should
operate (for objects) and how ownership is transferred (for both objects and
primitives) so that memory can be managed from the host
language runtime.

GObject also defines {b closures}, which allow the C-language code to
call back into the host language; and {b signals}, which are events
emitted on an object, that can be listened to via a closure. (You will
interact with signals by registering an OCaml callback function - 
closures are an internal detail).

{1 Overview: The two layers}

OCaml is a functional language with an object-oriented layer. Because
GTK is object-oriented but OCaml requires C-bindings to occur in its
functional / module layer, we provide two sets of bindings:

* {b Layer 2: classes and objects}: this is an object-oriented binding to GObject
* {b Layer 1: functions}: a pure functional binding to GObject

{@mermaid[
flowchart TD
  C["C library (libgtk-4, libgio-2.0, ...)"]
  L1["Layer 1: Gtk.Wrappers.Button
  type t, one function per C function"]
  L2["Layer 2: Gtk.Button
  class type button_t, class button, new_* constructors"]
  APP["Your code"]
  C --> L1 --> L2
  APP -- "usually" --> L2
  APP -. "interop, low level" .-> L1
]}

(Internally, the C binding layer is known as "Layer 0").

You can use each layer independently of the other one, but because the classes
layer wraps around the functional layer, you can also move back and forth as
needed.

{2 Class Types / Constructors}


{2 Modules / Functions}

You can also access GTK through a pure functional layer, where
the GObject instance is an opaque type you pass as the first parameter
of a function.
TODO: cover
- Layer 1 (the [Wrappers] modules): one module per class, an abstract
  handle type [t], one function per C function. Closest to the C API.
- Layer 2 (the class modules): one module per class, holding an OCaml
  class type, a class, and constructors. Methods, signals and properties
  are object methods. This is the layer most code should use.
- Both layers wrap the same objects; you can move between them (see
  {{!section-converting}Converting between the layers}).
- When to use Layer 1: interop, functions with no Layer 2 form, or
  avoiding objects in performance-sensitive code (check that this claim
  holds before writing it).


{1 Libraries and namespaces}

ocgtk is a single opam package, but internally it contains one
library per GObject namespace, such as `Ocgtk_gtk`.

{t | Namespace | Library |
   | --------- | ------- |
   | GTK       | Ocgtk_gtk |
   | Gdk       | Ocgtk_gdk |
   | Pango     | Ocgtk_pango |
   | Gio       | Ocgtk_gio |}

In each library, you'll find a layout like the following:

{[
Ocgtk_<namespace>
  +- <Namespace>             (* class and interface modules (layer 2) *)
  |    +- Wrappers           (* functional modules (layer 1) *)
  +- <Namespace>_enums       (* enumerations *)
  +- <Namespace>_constants   (* constants *)
]}

For example, GTK has a layout like the following:

{[
Ocgtk_gtk
  +- Gtk                     (* class and interface modules (layer 2) *)
  |    +- Wrappers           (* functional modules (layer 1) *)
  +- Gtk_enums
  +- Gtk_constants
  +- GMain                   (* GTK only: initialisation and main loop *)
  +- GVersion                (* GTK only: version information *)
]}

These are designed so that you can open [Ocgtk_gtk] and [Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk], and then refer to
each class module and [GMain] directly e.g.:

{[

open Ocgtk_gtk
open Gtk

let () =
  let _ = GMain.init () in
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  window#set_gravity `TOP_LEFT;
  window#set_default_size 300 200;
  GMain.main ()
]}

{1:anatomy Anatomy of a class module}

Known internally as "Layer 2", this is the recommended way of interacting 
with ocgtk. Each GObject class and interface is mapped to an OCaml class which you 
can invoke methods on, but you don't instantiate them directly.

Each class and its type is declared in a separate module. The module is named after the GObject class. It will also contain one or more constructor functions that instantiate an object of that class for you.

(Each class has been put in its own module for several reasons. The first is practicality: OCaml's compiler won't easily handle a file with hundreds of class types and classes, each with dozens of methods. By splitting them up, they can also be compiled in parallel (which is much faster), and unused classes won't get pulled into your application and bloat its binary size).

For example the [Gtk.Window] class, class type and constructors are available in the [Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk.Window]
module, which looks like this:

{@ocaml[
(* The class type, returned by a constructor function *)
class type window_t 

(* The class itself, never used directly (only if you are bridging L1 -> L2).
   It takes the Layer 1 handle, Wrappers.Window.t, which is
   [ `window | `widget | `initially_unowned | `object_ ] Gobject.obj *)
class window : Wrappers.Window.t -> window_t

(* Constructor, used to create a new instance *)
val new_ : unit -> window_t
]}

Class types are named in full so that they can be disambiguated in hover
and type-preview via LSP/merlin.

{@mermaid[
flowchart LR
  subgraph NS["Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk (namespace module)"]
    direction TB
    subgraph L2M["Button (Layer 2)"]
      CT["class type button_t
      methods, signals, properties"]
      CL["class button : Wrappers.Button.t -> button_t"]
      CTOR["new_ / new_with_label / ...
      return button_t"]
    end
    subgraph W["Wrappers"]
      L1M["Button (Layer 1)
      type t, new_, get_label, set_label, ..."]
    end
    ENUMS["Enumerations: polymorphic variants"]
    CONSTS["Constants"]
  end
  CTOR --> CL --> CT
  CL -. wraps .-> L1M
]}

{1 Creating objects}

Use a constructor in a class's module to instantiate it, for example:
{[

open Ocgtk_gtk
open Gtk

let () =
  let _ = GMain.init () in
  let window = Window.new_ () in
  ...
]}

{b Interfaces} are also classes in OCaml, but they won't have constructor functions. The same applies to {b abstract classes}. For both of these, you will get values indirectly as the result of calling methods on other objects.

{1 Inheritance and interfaces}

{2 How inheritance and interface implementation is implemented in OCaml}

Classes and Interfaces are represented as a class type and a class in OCaml. The OCaml class type for each GObject class/interface will inherit both its base class and each of its interfaces.

(Unlike GObject's single-inheritance + interfaces, OCaml supports multiple inheritance).

For example in GObject, the [Window] class inherits from [Widget] and implements [Accessible], [Buildable], [ConstraintTarget], [Native], [Root] and [ShortcutManager].

In OCaml, this will look something like:

{[
class type window_t = object
  (* base class *)
  inherit Widget.widget_t

  (* implements *)
  (* The other interfaces would appear here, but widget_t already inherits them, so they are skipped *)
  inherit Native.native_t
  inherit Root.root_t
  inherit ShortcutManager.shortcut_manager_t

end
]}

Note that [Widget] already implements [Accessible], [Buildable] and [ConstraintTarget], so they won't be added again to the definition of [Window].

{2 Upcasting: how to pass subclass values and use polymorphism}

Say you have a [button_t], but you want to pass it as a value to a method that
declares a parameter of [widget_t]. Unlike other object-oriented languages where you
can pass this directly, OCaml requires you to explicitly upcast it using the [:>]
operator.

For example, [Box#append] takes a [widget_t], so a button must be upcast before
it can be added to a box:

{[
open Ocgtk_gtk.Gtk

let build_layout () =
  let box = Box.new_ `VERTICAL 10 in
  let button = Button.new_with_label "Click me" in

  (* Correct: upcast with :> *)
  let () = box#append (button :> Widget.widget_t) in
  box
]}

Without the upcast, [box#append button] is a type error, because [button] has
type [button_t] but [append] expects a [widget_t].

The same applies wherever a method takes a [widget_t]. For example,
[window#set_child (Some (box :> Widget.widget_t))] passes a [box_t option] to a
parameter of type [widget_t option].

{2 Cyclic groups and top-level classes}

You may notice references to modules like [Application_and__window_and__window_group] in the documentation and the source code. This is because some GObject classes and/or interfaces have mutually recursive dependencies between them.

In OCaml, these can't be declared in separate files, but instead need to be declared in an OCaml recursive cluster in the same file i.e. [class type c1 ... and c2 .. end] / [class c1 ... and c2 ... end]. Each individual class is then aliased in a separate module to "flatten" the namespace and hide the recursive classes.

{1:naming Name mapping}

{2:classes Classes}

The following table shows how various things are mapped between GObject (in the GIR files and GObject documentation) and the equivalent:
* C declarations 
* OCaml for classes (Layer 2)  
* OCaml modules/functions (layer 1).

{t | Component | GObject | C | Class Layer (L2) | Module Layer (L1) |
   | ----------| ----------- | ----- |
   | Methods   | set_label | [gtk_button_set_label(b, "x")] | [b#set_label "x"] | [Wrappers.Button.set_label b "x"] |
   | Instance Type | Button | [GtkButton] | [Gtk.Button.button_t] | [Gtk.Wrappers.Button.t] |
   | Constructors | [gtk_button_new_with_label("x")] | [Gtk.Button.new_with_label "x"] | [Gtk.Wrappers.Button.new_with_label "x"] |
   | Signals | clicked | [g_signal_connect(button, "clicked", ...)] | [b#on_clicked ~callback:(fun () -> ...)] |  [Wrappers.Button.on_clicked b ~callback:(fun () -> ...)] |
   | Properties (setters) | icon-name | [g_value_set_string(&sv, "question"); g_object_set_property(G_OBJECT(b), "icon-name", sv)] | [b#set_icon_name "question"] | [Wrappers.Button.set_icon_name b "question"] |

|}

Other rules to note:
* When a GObject method or type is a reserved word in OCaml, it is suffixed with an additional underscore e.g. [new] -> [new_], [type] -> [type_]
* If there is a property whose name would conflict with a method when generated, the property accessor is suppressed as OCaml cannot have both, and the method (should) do the same thing.

{2:constants Constants}

Constants are a name for a fixed value in GObject (derived directly from the GIR).

You'll find all constants in the top-level [<Namespace>_constants] module (e.g. [Gtk_constants], converted to snake_case in OCaml. As they are just OCaml values, you can use them with both the classes layer and the module layer.

{t
   | GObject | C | OCaml |
   | ----------| ----------- | ----- |
| INPUT_AGE | | GTK_INPUT_AGE | Gtk_constants.input_age |
}

{2:enumerations Enumerations}

Enumerations are mapped into the top-level [<Namespace>_enums] module (e.g. [Gtk_enums]).
Each enumeration is a polymorphic variant type.

The enumeration type is mapped to lowercase without spaces e.g. [AccessibleProperty] in GIR becomes [type accessibleproperty] in OCaml.

Each property is kept as {b screaming snake case}

{t
   | GObject | C | OCaml variant constructor |
   | autocomplete |  GTK_ACCESSIBLE_PROPERTY_AUTOCOMPLETE | `AUTOCOMPLETE |
   | multi_selectable | GTK_ACCESSIBLE_PROPERTY_MULTI_SELECTABLE | `MULTI_SELECTABLE |
}

There are also converter functions to convert them to integer form ([<enum>_to_int] e.g. [accessibleproperty_to_int]) and from integer form ([<enum_of_int] e.g. [accessible_property_of_int]), the latter of which throws if you pass an unmapped value.

{2:bitfields Bitfields}

Bitfields are also mapped to the top-level [<Namespace>_enums] module (e.g. [Gtk_enums]).
A bitfield is represented in OCaml as a list, where each entry is a value of an associated polymorphic type.

For example, the GObject StyleContextPrintFlags bitfield will be declared as such:

{[

(* StyleContextPrintFlags values type *)
type stylecontextprintflags_flag = 
  [ `NONE | `RECURSE | `SHOW_STYLE | `SHOW_CHANGE ]

(* StyleContextPrintFlags type *)
type stylecontextprintflags = stylecontextprintflags_flag list

]}

Any parameter or return type or property will use the [stylecontextprintflags] type. At runtime, each value is mapped to an integer value and bitwise-or`ed together.

{1 Types}

{2 Nullable values}

Nullable values in GObject are represented as an OCaml [option] type.

{2 Error values}

Any GLib.Error type as a return value or parameter is mapped to `GError.t` in OCaml.

However, when a method is specified with the [throws] attribute, the OCaml return type will be an OCaml [result] type with [GError.t] as the [`err] part of the type. 

For example, [Gdk.Clipboard.read_value_finish] declares a return type of [GObject.Value], so it's signature becomes in OCaml:

{|
class clipboard_t : object
  ...
  method read_value_finish :
    Ocgtk_gio.Gio.Async_result.async_result_t ->
    (Gobject.Value.t, GError.t) result
end
|}

(in C, these functions take a [GError**] value to be able to return a newly allocated error object when there is an exception)

{2 Primitive Types}

{3 Strings}

OCaml strings are mapped transparently in parameters and return types. They are copied in full between GObject strings and OCaml strings in the C layer.

{3 Integers}

Plain C [int] and [uint] values ([gint], [guint]) are 32-bit and become ordinary OCaml
[int]. Out-of-range values are not checked.

Similarly, [gssize], [gulong] and [gchar] are converted to int and are not checked for out-of-range values. 

A [gint64] becomes an OCaml [int64] and maps with no range issues.

Fixed-width integers get their own types in [Ocgtk_common], so that the range
is part of the type and a value that doesn't fit is caught when you convert it:

{t | C type | OCaml type | Range |
   | ------ | ---------- | ----- |
   | [guint8]  | [UInt8.t]  | 0 to 255 |
   | [gint8]   | [Int8.t]   | -128 to 127 |
   | [guint16] | [UInt16.t] | 0 to 65535 |
   | [gint16]  | [Int16.t]  | -32768 to 32767 |
   | [guint32] | [UInt32.t] | 0 to 4294967295 |
   | [gint32]  | [Int32.t]  | -2147483648 to 2147483647 |
   | [guint64] | [UInt64.t] | 0 to 2{^64}-1 |
   | [gsize]   | [Gsize.t]  | 0 to [max_int] |}

Apart from [UInt64], these are all [private int], so they cost nothing at
runtime. You can read them as an [int] with [to_int], but you can only create
one through [of_int]. [UInt64.t] is abstract and holds an [int64] internally.

Each module has the same small API:

- [of_int : int -> t] raises [Invalid_argument] if the value is out of range.
- [to_int : t -> int] converts back.
- [min_int], [max_int] and [zero] (or [minus_one] for signed types) are constants of
  type [t]. [min_value] and [max_value] give the same limits as plain [int]s.

{[
let n = UInt16.of_int 300 in
let width : int = UInt16.to_int n + 1 in
ignore (UInt8.of_int 256) (* raises Invalid_argument *)
]}

[Int32] shadows [Stdlib.Int32] on some versions if Ocgtk_common is opened. If you need OCaml's boxed [int32], write
[Stdlib.Int32]. All the modules are also grouped under [Bounded_int], for
example [Bounded_int.UInt16].

